1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:06,094
Kentish Town police station, north London, in the early months of 1915.

2
00:00:06,094 --> 00:00:10,974
In a back room stands an ordinary household bath, filled with water.

3
00:00:10,974 --> 00:00:14,562
A young woman in a bathing costume is lying in it.

4
00:00:14,562 --> 00:00:18,867
A Scotland Yard detective is standing at the end of the tub.

5
00:00:19,318 --> 00:00:30,346
She is a volunteer, chosen because she is about the same height and build as a woman who died in a bath two and a half years earlier, at the seaside, in Kent.

6
00:00:30,346 --> 00:00:35,162
The detective has already tried to push her under the water by force.

7
00:00:35,162 --> 00:00:39,140
Each time, she struggled, splashed and grabbed the sides.

8
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:41,444
It was obvious, and it was noisy.

9
00:00:41,893 --> 00:00:46,730
Then, according to his own later account, he does something different.

10
00:00:46,730 --> 00:00:50,738
Without warning, he takes hold of her feet and lifts them.

11
00:00:50,738 --> 00:00:53,088
Her head slides under the surface.

12
00:00:53,088 --> 00:00:54,539
And she stops moving.

13
00:00:54,539 --> 00:01:00,414
It reportedly took the detective and a doctor around half an hour to bring her round.

14
00:01:00,863 --> 00:01:07,662
Three women had died in baths like this one, in three different towns, over two and a half years.

15
00:01:07,662 --> 00:01:10,535
Each death had been examined by a doctor.

16
00:01:10,535 --> 00:01:12,708
Each had gone before a coroner.

17
00:01:12,708 --> 00:01:15,230
And each had been ruled an accident.

18
00:01:15,682 --> 00:01:19,946
All three had married the same man, under three different names.

19
00:01:19,946 --> 00:01:31,408
And within months, that experiment in a police station would help put something in front of a British jury that it was rarely allowed to hear: not one death, but a pattern.

20
00:01:31,856 --> 00:01:33,647
This is Precinct Archive.

21
00:01:33,647 --> 00:01:45,962
This is the case of the Brides in the Bath, and how three bathtubs, a newspaper cutting and a pathologist's tape measure became a turning point in British forensic science.

22
00:01:47,813 --> 00:01:55,683
George Joseph Smith was born in Bethnal Green, in the East End of London, on the 11th of January, 1872.

23
00:01:55,683 --> 00:01:58,282
His father was an insurance agent.

24
00:01:58,282 --> 00:02:04,471
By the age of nine, the boy had been sent to a reformatory at Gravesend, in Kent.

25
00:02:04,471 --> 00:02:09,286
As a young man he was in and out of prison for theft and fraud.

26
00:02:09,735 --> 00:02:16,132
In 1896, he was sentenced to twelve months for persuading a woman to steal from her employers.

27
00:02:16,132 --> 00:02:21,304
It is the first appearance in the file of something that would matter later.

28
00:02:21,304 --> 00:02:23,209
Smith did not rob strangers.

29
00:02:23,209 --> 00:02:25,931
He worked through women who trusted him.

30
00:02:26,382 --> 00:02:30,363
With the proceeds he opened a baker's shop in Leicester.

31
00:02:30,363 --> 00:02:35,696
In 1898, calling himself George Oliver Love, he married Caroline Thornhill.

32
00:02:35,696 --> 00:02:39,251
It was the only legal marriage he would ever make.

33
00:02:39,251 --> 00:02:47,854
The couple moved to London, where she took jobs as a maid and, according to the record, stole from her employers for him.

34
00:02:48,305 --> 00:02:51,408
She was caught in Worthing and served a year.

35
00:02:51,408 --> 00:02:59,269
When she came out, she gave evidence against her husband, and in January 1901 he was sent to prison for two years.

36
00:02:59,269 --> 00:03:04,579
When he was released, Caroline left England for Canada, and out of his reach.

37
00:03:05,028 --> 00:03:12,012
Over the next six years, Smith would marry again and again, never once divorced.

38
00:03:12,012 --> 00:03:19,782
Between 1908 and 1914, he went through seven bigamous marriages, under a string of names.

39
00:03:19,782 --> 00:03:21,353
George Rose Smith.

40
00:03:21,353 --> 00:03:22,663
Henry Williams.

41
00:03:22,663 --> 00:03:24,496
Charles Oliver James.

42
00:03:24,496 --> 00:03:25,456
John Lloyd.

43
00:03:25,907 --> 00:03:27,729
The method rarely changed.

44
00:03:27,729 --> 00:03:32,144
In June 1908 he married Florence Wilson, a widow from Worthing.

45
00:03:32,144 --> 00:03:43,148
Within a few weeks he was gone, along with about thirty pounds from her savings and whatever of her belongings he could sell from their rooms in Camden Town.

46
00:03:43,598 --> 00:03:50,979
That same summer, in Bristol, he married Edith Pegler, who had answered his advertisement for a housekeeper.

47
00:03:50,979 --> 00:03:52,345
Edith was different.

48
00:03:52,345 --> 00:03:55,011
She was the one he kept coming back to.

49
00:03:55,011 --> 00:03:58,496
He told her he dealt in antiques and had to travel.

50
00:03:58,496 --> 00:04:02,119
He would disappear for months, and return with money.

51
00:04:02,568 --> 00:04:07,694
In October 1909, as George Rose Smith, he married Sarah Freeman.

52
00:04:07,694 --> 00:04:11,058
He emptied her savings and sold her bonds.

53
00:04:11,058 --> 00:04:15,223
Accounts put the total at about four hundred pounds.

54
00:04:15,223 --> 00:04:16,584
Then he vanished.

55
00:04:16,584 --> 00:04:21,390
Up to this point, the file describes a bigamist and a thief.

56
00:04:21,390 --> 00:04:24,354
Nothing in it yet describes a killer.

57
00:04:24,803 --> 00:04:34,192
In 1910, in the seaside town of Weymouth, in Dorset, a man calling himself Henry Williams met a woman named Beatrice Mundy.

58
00:04:34,192 --> 00:04:36,253
Everyone called her Bessie.

59
00:04:36,253 --> 00:04:42,436
She was in her early thirties, and, unlike his earlier wives, she had real money.

60
00:04:42,887 --> 00:04:50,552
Her father, reportedly a bank manager, had left her a sum that accounts put at about two and a half thousand pounds.

61
00:04:50,552 --> 00:04:55,905
But it was held in trust by her family, and paid out to her as a small allowance.

62
00:04:55,905 --> 00:04:58,152
She could not simply hand it over.

63
00:04:58,152 --> 00:05:00,597
They married in Weymouth that summer.

64
00:05:01,048 --> 00:05:08,025
When it became clear that the capital could not be reached, Williams left her, taking what cash he could.

65
00:05:08,025 --> 00:05:09,553
Hold on to that detail.

66
00:05:09,553 --> 00:05:15,268
The money in trust was the one obstacle he could not get round while Bessie was alive.

67
00:05:15,268 --> 00:05:18,524
There was one way round it, and it needed a will.

68
00:05:18,973 --> 00:05:22,810
For almost two years, Bessie heard nothing from him.

69
00:05:22,810 --> 00:05:29,968
Then, in 1912, she was staying in Weston-super-Mare when, by chance, she met him on the seafront.

70
00:05:29,968 --> 00:05:33,214
Whatever he said to her that day, it worked.

71
00:05:33,214 --> 00:05:34,543
She took him back.

72
00:05:34,992 --> 00:05:43,045
By the early summer they had taken a house at 80 High Street, in Herne Bay, a small resort on the north Kent coast.

73
00:05:43,045 --> 00:05:44,866
The house had no bathroom.

74
00:05:44,866 --> 00:05:51,659
Accounts say that Williams went to a local ironmonger and bought a bath, haggling over the price.

75
00:05:52,108 --> 00:05:58,312
In early July 1912, Bessie and her husband made wills, each in favour of the other.

76
00:05:58,312 --> 00:06:03,768
A few days later, Williams took his wife to a local doctor, Frank French.

77
00:06:03,768 --> 00:06:05,861
She complained of headaches.

78
00:06:05,861 --> 00:06:13,111
It was her husband who described fits, and the doctor who wrote down a possible case of epilepsy.

79
00:06:13,562 --> 00:06:21,667
On the night of Friday, the 12th of July, Williams went to fetch Dr French, saying his wife had had another seizure.

80
00:06:21,667 --> 00:06:24,812
French saw her, and said he would call again.

81
00:06:24,812 --> 00:06:30,332
The next morning, Saturday the 13th, he was told that Bessie Williams was dead.

82
00:06:30,781 --> 00:06:40,358
French found her in the bath, her head under the water, her legs stretched out straight, her feet resting above the surface at the far end.

83
00:06:40,358 --> 00:06:42,424
There was no sign of violence.

84
00:06:42,424 --> 00:06:45,524
He put the drowning down to an epileptic fit.

85
00:06:45,524 --> 00:06:46,833
The inquest agreed.

86
00:06:46,833 --> 00:06:51,863
Her estate, the money that had been held in trust, passed to her husband.

87
00:06:52,312 --> 00:06:58,873
That position in the bath would turn out to be one of the most important facts in the whole case.

88
00:06:58,873 --> 00:07:00,970
Nobody saw it that way in 1912.

89
00:07:00,970 --> 00:07:07,463
Henry Williams disappeared from Herne Bay, and went back to Edith Pegler in Bristol, with money.

90
00:07:09,314 --> 00:07:20,161
Late in 1913, now using his own name, George Smith married Alice Burnham, a young nurse from Buckinghamshire, at a register office in Portsmouth.

91
00:07:20,161 --> 00:07:24,949
Her father, Charles Burnham, did not take to his new son-in-law.

92
00:07:24,949 --> 00:07:29,663
Hold on to that, too, because Charles Burnham would not let go.

93
00:07:30,112 --> 00:07:39,284
Shortly after the wedding, a life insurance policy was taken out on Alice, worth about five hundred pounds, with her husband to benefit.

94
00:07:39,284 --> 00:07:45,421
In December the couple went north, to Blackpool, for what was described as a short holiday.

95
00:07:45,870 --> 00:07:51,726
Accounts say the first lodgings they looked at were turned down because there was no bath.

96
00:07:51,726 --> 00:07:57,842
They took rooms instead at a boarding house in Regent Road, kept by Joseph and Alice Crossley.

97
00:07:57,842 --> 00:08:03,112
One account of the case says Smith looked at the bath before they agreed to stay.

98
00:08:03,562 --> 00:08:09,046
On the evening of Friday, the 12th of December, 1913, Alice took a bath.

99
00:08:09,046 --> 00:08:11,559
It was her husband who found her.

100
00:08:11,559 --> 00:08:13,083
A doctor was called.

101
00:08:13,083 --> 00:08:20,091
The inquest found that she had drowned by accident, perhaps after fainting in the hot water.

102
00:08:20,091 --> 00:08:22,909
A newspaper in Blackpool reported it.

103
00:08:22,909 --> 00:08:28,012
Smith, reportedly, arranged the cheapest burial on offer, and left.

104
00:08:28,463 --> 00:08:29,520
A year passed.

105
00:08:29,520 --> 00:08:37,454
In December 1914, in the city of Bath, a man calling himself John Lloyd married Margaret Elizabeth Lofty.

106
00:08:37,454 --> 00:08:42,290
She was thirty-eight, and, accounts say, a clergyman's daughter.

107
00:08:42,290 --> 00:08:49,543
Three days before the wedding, she had taken out a life policy worth about seven hundred pounds.

108
00:08:49,993 --> 00:08:57,149
Straight after the wedding, the couple took lodgings at 14 Bismarck Road, in Highgate, north London.

109
00:08:57,149 --> 00:09:04,950
On the 18th of December, Margaret went to a solicitor and made a new will, leaving everything to her husband.

110
00:09:04,950 --> 00:09:07,097
She also withdrew her savings.

111
00:09:07,097 --> 00:09:10,604
It was a little over three hours before she died.

112
00:09:11,053 --> 00:09:13,010
That evening she took a bath.

113
00:09:13,010 --> 00:09:24,409
According to the way the landlady's evidence is usually told, she heard splashing from the bathroom, then quiet, and then the sound of the harmonium in the sitting room.

114
00:09:24,409 --> 00:09:29,131
Shortly afterwards, Mr Lloyd went out, came back, and called for help.

115
00:09:29,131 --> 00:09:31,559
His wife was found dead in the bath.

116
00:09:32,009 --> 00:09:39,708
The coroner's doctor found almost nothing: no sign of a struggle, only a tiny bruise above the left elbow.

117
00:09:39,708 --> 00:09:42,104
The verdict was accidental death.

118
00:09:42,104 --> 00:09:43,339
It was the third.

119
00:09:43,339 --> 00:09:48,859
And this time, a newspaper carried the story a long way beyond north London.

120
00:09:49,308 --> 00:09:59,893
Shortly before Christmas, the News of the World printed a short report of the death of Mrs Margaret Lloyd, found in her bath at her lodgings in Highgate.

121
00:09:59,893 --> 00:10:02,937
In Buckinghamshire, Charles Burnham read it.

122
00:10:02,937 --> 00:10:05,565
So, in Blackpool, did Joseph Crossley.

123
00:10:05,565 --> 00:10:07,987
Both were struck by the same thing.

124
00:10:08,437 --> 00:10:17,203
On the 3rd of January, 1915, Crossley wrote to the Metropolitan Police, on behalf of himself, his wife and Alice's father.

125
00:10:17,203 --> 00:10:27,910
He enclosed two cuttings: the report of Margaret Lloyd's death, and the report of the Blackpool inquest into the death of Alice Smith a year earlier.

126
00:10:27,910 --> 00:10:31,503
The two deaths, he wrote, were strikingly similar.

127
00:10:31,952 --> 00:10:37,392
The letter reached Divisional Detective Inspector Arthur Neil, at Kentish Town.

128
00:10:37,392 --> 00:10:38,425
It was wartime.

129
00:10:38,425 --> 00:10:44,760
Neil's division was already busy with enemy aliens and the other duties of a country at war.

130
00:10:44,760 --> 00:10:52,955
A link between two accidental drownings, more than two hundred miles apart, could easily have been filed and forgotten.

131
00:10:53,405 --> 00:10:57,606
Instead, Neil went to Bismarck Road and looked at the bath.

132
00:10:57,606 --> 00:11:07,502
He found it hard to believe that a grown woman could have drowned by accident in a tub that small, a tub that had been three-quarters full.

133
00:11:07,502 --> 00:11:14,407
That is an inference, not a fact from the file, but it is the inference that started the inquiry.

134
00:11:14,857 --> 00:11:17,441
Then the file gave him something firmer.

135
00:11:17,441 --> 00:11:25,452
On the 12th of January, the coroner's doctor told Neil that he had received an enquiry from the Yorkshire Insurance Company.

136
00:11:25,452 --> 00:11:28,230
Mr Lloyd was claiming on his wife's policy.

137
00:11:28,230 --> 00:11:35,206
Neil asked the doctor to hold his reply, and requested everything Blackpool had on the death of Alice Smith.

138
00:11:35,657 --> 00:11:38,961
Blackpool's answer made the pattern sharper.

139
00:11:38,961 --> 00:11:41,589
Alice, too, had been newly married.

140
00:11:41,589 --> 00:11:47,145
Alice, too, had been insured, and had made a will in her husband's favour.

141
00:11:47,145 --> 00:11:50,374
Both men had found their wives in the bath.

142
00:11:50,374 --> 00:11:52,627
Both had left soon afterwards.

143
00:11:52,627 --> 00:11:59,610
And from the descriptions, Mr Smith of Blackpool and Mr Lloyd of Highgate sounded very alike.

144
00:12:00,062 --> 00:12:01,407
Neil now set a trap.

145
00:12:01,407 --> 00:12:07,796
He asked the doctor to send the insurers a favourable report, so that the claim would go ahead.

146
00:12:07,796 --> 00:12:15,800
He was counting on Mr Lloyd to call at his solicitor's office to collect, and he had the office watched, day and night.

147
00:12:15,800 --> 00:12:17,616
Would the man come himself?

148
00:12:19,466 --> 00:12:24,996
On the 1st of February, 1915, a man matching the description arrived.

149
00:12:24,996 --> 00:12:29,163
Neil stepped forward and asked if he was John Lloyd.

150
00:12:29,163 --> 00:12:30,365
He said he was.

151
00:12:30,365 --> 00:12:34,292
Neil then asked whether he was also George Smith.

152
00:12:34,292 --> 00:12:36,376
The man denied it, firmly.

153
00:12:36,376 --> 00:12:40,784
Neil told him he was being held on suspicion of bigamy.

154
00:12:40,784 --> 00:12:45,353
Before long, he admitted that he was indeed George Smith.

155
00:12:45,802 --> 00:12:48,485
Bigamy was a charge Neil could prove.

156
00:12:48,485 --> 00:12:50,369
Murder was another matter.

157
00:12:50,369 --> 00:12:54,211
Two coroners had already ruled the deaths accidental.

158
00:12:54,211 --> 00:12:58,560
There were no witnesses, no weapon, no marks worth the name.

159
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:05,447
If the case was going to be made, it would have to be made from the bodies, and from the baths.

160
00:13:05,897 --> 00:13:08,742
The Home Office turned to Bernard Spilsbury.

161
00:13:08,742 --> 00:13:18,379
He was thirty-seven, and already known from the Crippen trial five years earlier, where his evidence about a scar had helped convict a man of murder.

162
00:13:18,379 --> 00:13:20,578
Margaret Lloyd's body was exhumed.

163
00:13:21,028 --> 00:13:27,135
Spilsbury confirmed the small bruise above the elbow, and found two other tiny marks.

164
00:13:27,135 --> 00:13:32,740
He found no heart disease and no illness that might explain a sudden collapse.

165
00:13:32,740 --> 00:13:35,543
Even the signs of drowning were slight.

166
00:13:35,543 --> 00:13:39,567
Everything suggested that death had come almost at once.

167
00:13:39,567 --> 00:13:42,154
He had the organs tested for poison.

168
00:13:42,154 --> 00:13:43,232
There was none.

169
00:13:43,682 --> 00:13:55,808
By now the newspapers were running stories about the brides in the bath, and on the 8th of February a report arrived from the senior police officer in Herne Bay.

170
00:13:55,808 --> 00:14:01,306
It described the death of a Mrs Bessie Williams, in a bath, in July 1912.

171
00:14:01,306 --> 00:14:04,770
Neil sent photographs of his prisoner to Kent.

172
00:14:04,770 --> 00:14:08,837
The answer came back: Henry Williams was George Smith.

173
00:14:09,288 --> 00:14:10,186
Three women.

174
00:14:10,186 --> 00:14:12,282
Three names for the husband.

175
00:14:12,282 --> 00:14:14,304
Three verdicts of accident.

176
00:14:14,304 --> 00:14:26,058
Spilsbury went north to Blackpool to examine Alice Smith, and found the same picture as in Highgate: no violence, sudden death, very little sign of drowning.

177
00:14:26,509 --> 00:14:28,775
Then he examined Bessie Mundy.

178
00:14:28,775 --> 00:14:41,391
Here, after more than two years, he found one clear sign that she had drowned: goose flesh on the skin of her thigh, a sign associated with a sudden plunge into water.

179
00:14:41,391 --> 00:14:45,244
The question was no longer whether she had drowned.

180
00:14:45,244 --> 00:14:46,074
It was how.

181
00:14:46,524 --> 00:14:49,193
The three baths were brought to London.

182
00:14:49,193 --> 00:14:52,477
Spilsbury measured them, and measured the women.

183
00:14:52,477 --> 00:14:55,487
Bessie had been five feet seven inches tall.

184
00:14:55,487 --> 00:14:58,703
The bath in Herne Bay was about five feet long.

185
00:14:58,703 --> 00:15:03,765
And he thought hard about what an epileptic fit actually does to the body.

186
00:15:04,215 --> 00:15:09,976
In the first stage of a fit, he reasoned, the whole body goes rigid and stretches out.

187
00:15:09,976 --> 00:15:18,753
In a short bath with a sloping end, that would push a tall woman's head and shoulders up the slope, out of the water, not under it.

188
00:15:18,753 --> 00:15:21,031
In the next stage, the limbs jerk.

189
00:15:21,031 --> 00:15:25,452
Nothing in that sequence would slide her head beneath the surface.

190
00:15:25,903 --> 00:15:34,454
Now recall where Dr French had found her: her head under the water, her legs out straight, her feet raised at the narrow end.

191
00:15:34,454 --> 00:15:39,996
Spilsbury's conclusion was that this position was very hard to reach by accident.

192
00:15:39,996 --> 00:15:47,454
But if someone at the foot of the bath suddenly lifted her feet, her head and body would slide under at once.

193
00:15:47,903 --> 00:15:56,536
The sudden rush of water into the nose and throat, he suggested, could cause shock and almost immediate loss of consciousness.

194
00:15:56,536 --> 00:15:57,359
No struggle.

195
00:15:57,359 --> 00:15:58,112
No bruises.

196
00:15:58,112 --> 00:16:00,510
Few of the usual signs of drowning.

197
00:16:00,510 --> 00:16:07,704
It was a theory that explained the very absence of evidence that had made all three deaths look innocent.

198
00:16:08,154 --> 00:16:09,909
It still had to be tested.

199
00:16:09,909 --> 00:16:13,619
This brings the file back to that room in Kentish Town.

200
00:16:13,619 --> 00:16:20,838
Neil's volunteers, of similar build to the dead women, struggled visibly when he tried to force them under.

201
00:16:20,838 --> 00:16:26,775
When he lifted the feet, one of them went under without a fight, and lost consciousness.

202
00:16:26,775 --> 00:16:31,700
It is Neil's own account, and it is reported, not independently recorded.

203
00:16:31,700 --> 00:16:34,668
But it matched what Spilsbury had predicted.

204
00:16:35,119 --> 00:16:44,995
On the 23rd of March, 1915, George Joseph Smith was formally charged with the murders of Bessie Mundy, Alice Burnham and Margaret Lofty.

205
00:16:44,995 --> 00:16:50,151
But under English law he could be tried for only one of them at a time.

206
00:16:50,151 --> 00:16:53,492
The prosecution chose Bessie Mundy, the first.

207
00:16:53,492 --> 00:16:55,961
And that created the real problem.

208
00:16:55,961 --> 00:16:59,883
Would the jury be allowed to hear about the other two?

209
00:17:01,732 --> 00:17:08,410
The trial opened at the Old Bailey on the 22nd of June, 1915, before Mr Justice Scrutton.

210
00:17:08,410 --> 00:17:16,964
For the Crown, Archibald Bodkin, later Director of Public Prosecutions, with Travers Humphreys and Cecil Whiteley.

211
00:17:16,964 --> 00:17:22,892
For the defence, the most famous advocate of the day, Sir Edward Marshall Hall.

212
00:17:23,342 --> 00:17:27,144
Marshall Hall's first battle was over the other two deaths.

213
00:17:27,144 --> 00:17:35,136
A man on trial for one killing, he argued, should be judged on the evidence of that killing, not on the suspicion of others.

214
00:17:35,136 --> 00:17:43,064
If the jury heard about Blackpool and Highgate, the verdict on Herne Bay would be decided before a word of it was examined.

215
00:17:43,515 --> 00:17:44,916
The judge disagreed.

216
00:17:44,916 --> 00:17:59,076
The deaths in Blackpool and Highgate, he ruled, could be heard, not to prove that Smith had killed those women, but to show a system: to answer the claim that what happened in Herne Bay was an accident.

217
00:17:59,076 --> 00:18:02,090
One drowning in a bath might be misfortune.

218
00:18:02,090 --> 00:18:05,876
The same circumstances three times was something else.

219
00:18:06,326 --> 00:18:09,270
So the jury heard all three stories.

220
00:18:09,270 --> 00:18:21,375
Accounts put the number of prosecution witnesses at more than a hundred: landladies, doctors, bank clerks, insurance men, an ironmonger, registrars.

221
00:18:21,375 --> 00:18:30,045
One by one, they laid out the same sequence: a marriage, a will or a policy, a bath, a death, a departure.

222
00:18:30,494 --> 00:18:33,289
Spilsbury's evidence was the centre of it.

223
00:18:33,289 --> 00:18:39,211
Calm and certain, he took the jury through the measurements and the position of the body.

224
00:18:39,211 --> 00:18:44,135
A death like Bessie's, he said in effect, could not be explained by a fit.

225
00:18:44,135 --> 00:18:48,127
The bath from Herne Bay, it is reported, was there in court.

226
00:18:48,576 --> 00:18:50,579
Smith did not give evidence.

227
00:18:50,579 --> 00:18:55,515
His decision survives in a handwritten note he passed to his counsel.

228
00:18:55,515 --> 00:19:05,242
Marshall Hall did what he could: he attacked the idea of system, and argued that suspicion multiplied by three was still only suspicion.

229
00:19:05,693 --> 00:19:09,082
On the 1st of July, 1915, the jury retired.

230
00:19:09,082 --> 00:19:12,156
They were out for about twenty minutes.

231
00:19:12,156 --> 00:19:16,333
The verdict was guilty of the murder of Bessie Mundy.

232
00:19:16,333 --> 00:19:19,721
George Joseph Smith was sentenced to death.

233
00:19:20,171 --> 00:19:27,433
Marshall Hall appealed on the single ground that the evidence of system should never have been admitted.

234
00:19:27,433 --> 00:19:33,508
The Court of Criminal Appeal, under the Lord Chief Justice, Lord Reading, dismissed it.

235
00:19:33,508 --> 00:19:38,116
On the 13th of August, 1915, Smith was hanged at Maidstone Prison.

236
00:19:39,967 --> 00:19:42,624
The case is remembered for two firsts.

237
00:19:42,624 --> 00:19:52,205
In law, it became one of the first and best-known cases in which striking similarities between connected deaths were used to prove guilt.

238
00:19:52,205 --> 00:20:02,485
The principle of system evidence would be argued over in British courts for decades, and it was later used, and criticised, in other murder trials.

239
00:20:02,934 --> 00:20:06,783
In forensic science, it made Bernard Spilsbury famous.

240
00:20:06,783 --> 00:20:13,056
He was not only telling a court what caused a death, but reconstructing how it happened.

241
00:20:13,056 --> 00:20:16,477
Later pathologists were more cautious about him.

242
00:20:16,477 --> 00:20:21,467
One called him very brilliant, but fallible, and very, very obstinate.

243
00:20:21,467 --> 00:20:24,817
Some of his later evidence has been questioned.

244
00:20:24,817 --> 00:20:28,665
The conviction in this case has never been overturned.

245
00:20:29,115 --> 00:20:36,147
And at the centre of it are three women who did nothing more than trust a husband.

246
00:20:36,147 --> 00:20:39,234
Bessie Mundy, in Herne Bay, in 1912.

247
00:20:39,234 --> 00:20:42,406
Alice Burnham, in Blackpool, in 1913.

248
00:20:42,406 --> 00:20:45,579
Margaret Lofty, in Highgate, in 1914.

249
00:20:45,579 --> 00:20:48,837
They are remembered here with respect.

250
00:20:49,288 --> 00:20:54,896
Their story was not uncovered by a detective's hunch, or by a scientist alone.

251
00:20:54,896 --> 00:21:05,537
It began with a father who did not trust his son-in-law, and a boarding-house keeper in Blackpool who read a newspaper and decided to post a letter.

252
00:21:05,537 --> 00:21:07,335
This case file is closed.

253
00:21:07,335 --> 00:21:11,073
New case files arrive every day on Precinct Archive.
